enji Marshall throws a  dummy at exactly the right time and  makes a line break it looks it sounds  really easy when got the ASAP sings  somebody that I used to know right he  makes it look very easy but it's another  thing entirely and you you find out when  you actually try it yourself that you  know it's not as easy as it looks  you know you burn the cannelloni your  dummy part is completely unconvincing  and you know your voice cracks when you  try to hit the octave jump in the core  and the point is that you know you learn  your areas of weakness when you actually  test them and difficult exams test them  more than easy ones that make sense so  when you come out of an easy exam you  might think ah hey I'm not so bad after  all that may be true but it doesn't help  you to learn anything you know it  doesn't show you that when you've got a  locus and you've got to square root  expressions from distance equations on  one side of the equation  you can't just square everything out and  hope it's going to come out in the wash  you've got to rearrange things before  you do that or you know when you do the  easy exam it doesn't reveal that if you  don't read the question even it's the  very first paper very very first  question on the paper you're not going  to get the question right because you  weren't paying attention right so  difficult exams will help you learn  because they review your weaknesses  right and so we should learn to in fact  appreciate them let me illustrate this  another way and I owe this illustration  actually to a standard student named Chi  Y so you know if you're out there  watching Gy thanks for this one Jiro is  a runner and sometimes some mornings I  would see him practicing Sprint's out on  the oval and you could recognize him a  mile away because he did something very  unusual when he was sprinting he  actually wore a parachute he strapped a  parachute onto his back and so when he  ran you know it would kick up because of  the air resistance and it would make him  run slower okay now why did he do that  why why would he go to the effort of  making things harder for himself and  he's not the only one I have friends who  play basketball when they train they  strap weights onto their ankles as  they're playing basketball why do they  do that well I take it the reason why  the parachutes and the weights are  something they do to themselves is  because they're voluntarily making  things harder for themselves because  making it harder for themselves  I also get stronger and faster and  enables them when they take off those  constraints to just totally blow away  the competition and perform in a much  higher level  right and hard exams are the same  pushing you to your limits so that when  you do exams which you know when you get  to the actual HSC you'll be fine your  Blitzer right because you've had the  difficult practice okay reason number  four and this is the reason no one likes  to hear but difficult that that  experience that difficulty that you  finally do difficult is relative its  relative okay  if people come out of an exam saying oh  that was crazy you know that was so hard  I felt a rage quitting after ten minutes  okay let's think about this for a second  there are two main plausible reasons why  you had that experience with the exam  now firstly the exam could just have  been really hard okay that's definitely  a possibility there's not the only one  secondly it could be that you and your  grade are just much weaker than you  realize okay now these two aren't  mutually exclusive they can happen at  the same time but I hope you see the  point that when an exam feels difficult  who says it was a difficult exam it  really isn't it possible could it be  that if another graders had this exam  they might not find it difficult  they might just find it average right  couldn't it be that the grade as a whole  just really needs to pull their socks up  and work harder you know the more  difficult questions and complete the  assigned work that we give you you know  couldn't be that really the exam was  just normal and it's about your level of  competence not just that if you were to  the exam now that being said I don't  think the exams you two are easy okay  that's not our intention that sort of in  trying to explain why we do that right  but don't make excuses and don't just  assume it's the exams fault right if you  find the exam difficult don't just  question the exam question yourself as  well right be sobered by the fact that  your marks or a bit lower than your  expected you know there was this  sometimes I something that really  frustrates us is we give you questions  that coincidentally turn up in the exam  the exact same question maybe the  numbers are different right and you  still do poorly on them  well we give you specific advice you  know say in the post-mortem after an  exam and the following exam you continue  to make the same errors right now we all  do that raw human but that's that's  indicating that the fold is not the exam  it's it's that you've got to work hard  at your revision and the effort that  you're putting into it okay so I don't  just blame the exam question yourself as  well because difficulty is relative okay  so um let me review number one I'm war  marks are meaningless number two  easy exams don't sort you right so they  help hard exams help us to tell you  apart number three hard exams help you  to learn now before difficulty is  relative between you know grades and  years lastly most importantly exams as a  whole they're not the most important  thing in life okay  exams are way down the list of the  things that should be important to you  and so for me the one of the biggest  things is that those who respond  negatively to these exams do so almost  universally because they place too much  emphasis on the exam I've seen exams  I've written exams where people leave  the exam hall may shed tears afterwards  okay now I don't mean 